# 2026-10-24 (Build24): keep the SMDT PMU MCU watchdog alive — docs/45.
# 2026-10-29 (§31): add the missing other half — disarm it on shutdown.
#
# The MCU has two independent timers (docs/45 §30, both measured on this board):
#   1) boot-confirm: armed at power-on, ~620 s, needs no enable and no feeding.
#      If the SoC never reports boot completion, the MCU cuts power.
#   2) runtime watchdog: armed ONLY by notify (0x51<-0x33), ~78 s, must be fed
#      (0x33<-0xAB) once per second.
#
# The factory kernel does all of this in drivers/smdt/mcuinf.c: probe writes
# 0x32=1, the init trigger runs `mcu_tool notify_power_on`, the mcu_run_thread
# kthread feeds every 1000 ms, and .shutdown writes 0x32=0. That driver is
# missing from our kernel tree (the DTS node mcuinf@62 exists, so /dev/McuCom
# never appears), hence mcu_smdt reproduces the same I2C transfers from
# userspace.
#
# Deliberately NOT oneshot: if the feeder ever dies, init must bring it back.
# An armed-but-unfed watchdog cuts power ~78 s later (measured 2026-10-29;
# earlier notes said 155 s, which was the uptime at detection, not the window),
# and that restart has to be self-healing.
#
# 0x32 is retained across power loss in the MCU, so the daemon re-asserts
# 0x32=1 on every boot; without it the recovery layer would stay disabled
# (docs/45 §25).

service mcu_notify /vendor/bin/mcu_smdt --daemon --ms 1000
    class main
    user root
    stdio_to_kmsg
    disabled

on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    start mcu_notify

# 2026-10-29: shutdown handshake (docs/45 §31).
#
# The factory .shutdown callback writes 32=0; Build24 only implemented the
# "arm" half, so the watchdog stayed armed through shutdown and loader:
# `adb reboot loader` got cut ~80 s later, and a plain reboot had only ~54 s
# of margin (docs/45 §30). This restores the symmetric half.
#
# `exec` is synchronous: init waits for it to exit. Shutdown takes 5.7 s end to
# end on this board, so "disarm first, then power down" leaves a tiny window.
#
# Disarming only clears timer (2). Timer (1) does not depend on 0x32, so even
# though 0x32=0 survives the power cut, a failed boot is still cut at ~620 s
# (see docs/45 30.7).
on shutdown
    exec - root -- /vendor/bin/mcu_smdt --set 0x32 0
